---
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="About"
  metaTitle="About this site"
  description="What this site is, what it deliberately leaves out, and how it is built. No accounts, no tracking, no third-party scripts."
  path="/about"
  lastUpdated="2026-08-20"
  keywords={['about', 'fitness calculator site']}
>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is a set of health and fitness calculators built on published equations. Every calculator
    names the paper its formula comes from, shows the formula itself, and states what the equation assumes
    and where it is weak.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>

  <p>
    Most calculator sites give you a number and no way to check it. Some cite papers that do not
    contain the values they use. Several still compute standards that were withdrawn years ago. The
    number arrives with no indication of how much confidence it deserves.
  </p>

  <p>
    This site takes the opposite approach. Where equations disagree, all of them are shown. Where an
    equation is known to misbehave — the James lean mass equation turns over above about 150 kg, the
    Epley formula is not exact at a single repetition — the page says so. Where a widely used value
    has no published source, the page says that too.
  </p>

  <h2>What it will not do</h2>

  <p>
    There are limits built into the calculators themselves, not just the copy. Calorie targets are
    floored and deficits capped. No plan is produced toward a goal weight below a healthy BMI. No
    calorie or body-composition figure is produced for anyone under 18. These are enforced in code
    and covered by tests, so they cannot be lost in a redesign. The
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> sets out the full list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some calculators are deliberately absent. Kidney function, body surface area for chemotherapy
    dosing and blood alcohol content all produce numbers that feed real decisions, and they belong
    with a clinician. Pregnancy and fertility calculators are the highest-scrutiny category in
    health, properly served by established medical publishers.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>

  <p>
    Static pages, no accounts, no database. Nothing you type is sent anywhere — the calculations run
    in your browser, and the site has no server to send them to. There are no analytics, no
    advertising scripts and no third-party requests of any kind. Fonts are served from this domain.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your unit preference and theme choice are stored in your browser's local storage so the site
    remembers them. That is the only thing stored, it never leaves your device, and clearing your
    browser data removes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Accuracy and corrections</h2>

  <p>
    Every formula is checked against worked examples from its source paper in an automated test
    suite. That catches transcription errors, which are the most common way a calculator ends up
    quietly wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    It does not catch everything. If you find an error — a wrong coefficient, a misattributed
    citation, a standard that has been superseded — <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. Corrections to
    formulas are the most valuable thing anyone can send.
  </p>

  <h2>Not medical advice</h2>

  <p>
    These are estimates from population-level equations. They describe averages across groups of
    people, and individual needs vary in ways no equation captures. Nothing here is medical advice.
    If you have concerns about eating, weight or body image, a doctor or a qualified professional is
    the right place to take them.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
